{ignore}
<p><code>{tree 'menu'}</code> renders its content - the body - once for every top row, like
any data tag. The difference is inside the body: <a href="?tag/recurse">{recurse}</a> renders
that same body again for the children of the current row, each child a row of its own, and so
on down to any depth. Menus, category trees, folder listings and threaded comments are all
one body written once.</p>

<p>The children of a row are the array in its <code>children</code> field; the
<code>children='items'</code> option names another field, used at every depth. A row
without children - the field missing or empty - renders nothing at its <code>{recurse}</code>.
<a href="?tag/branch">{branch}</a> ... <code>{/branch}</code> renders only for a row that has
children, so the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> around a <code>{recurse}</code> is left out for a
leaf, and its <code>@else@</code> part renders for a leaf instead.</p>

<p>Every list of the tree is a level named as the tree is: <code>depth@tree</code> is 1 for the
top rows and one more per level down, and <code>first@tree</code>, <code>current@tree</code>,
<code>count@tree</code> speak of the list being rendered. The handling options
<code>sort</code>, <code>where</code> and <code>reverse</code> written on the tree apply to
every list, the children included. The <code>@else@</code> part of the tree itself shows when
there are no rows at all.</p>

<p>Under the strict syntax check a tree without rows named, a name nothing holds, or a tree
without its closing tag is an error.</p>
{/ignore}
